  "question": "If $\\sin^{-1}x + \\sin^{-1}y = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, then $\\cos^{-1}x + \\cos^{-1}y$ equals:",
  "answeroption": "A",
  "explanation": "Let $x = \\sin\\alpha$ and $y = \\sin\\beta$. Then $\\alpha + \\beta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.<br /><br />Thus $\\beta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\alpha$.<br /><br />Now $\\cos^{-1}x = \\cos^{-1}(\\sin\\alpha) = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\alpha$.<br /><br />Also $\\cos^{-1}y = \\cos^{-1}(\\sin\\beta) = \\cos^{-1}(\\cos\\alpha) = \\alpha$.<br /><br />Hence $\\cos^{-1}x + \\cos^{-1}y = (\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\alpha) + \\alpha = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
